00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.150
Chris: So, what is a Deployment strategy?

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.150
A ‘deployment strategy’ is a process to
change or upgrade a software product.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.479
One of the aims of a ‘deployment strategy’, is to
release a new version without downtime, so that

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.070
the user is free to continue using the product.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.730
One of the more common strategies is the
‘blue-green’ deployment strategy but there are

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.180
several different strategies that can be used

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.250
Let’s have a look at these
in a little more detail.

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:33.830
Leah: Basic deployment is fast,
simple, and cost-effective.

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:38.730
To create a new version, it does a full
update of the product in the environment

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.560
simultaneously, This process makes it
vulnerable, more difficult to update, and this

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.430
strategy would make it harder to rollback.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.500
It carries the highest risk
and would be unsuitable for

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.729
business-critical products or services.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:56.430
Chris: The blue-green deployment
strategy would have two versions of

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.519
a software product at the same time.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.040
The new ‘blue’ version goes through testing
and evaluation while the user continues

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.120
to use the stable ‘green’ version.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.250
The user will be switched to the
‘blue’ version when it is ready.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.950
This is a good strategy to use, as
if there is a problem, it can be

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.590
switched back to the ‘green’ version.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.699
One way to think of this is how it would look in
a source [version] code control environment such

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:28.310
as ‘Git’ or ‘GitHub’, there would be a ‘Staging
branch’ which would be the ‘blue’ version, and a

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.850
Master branch which would be the ‘green’ version.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:38.229
Leah: Multi-service deployments are
also simple, fast, cheap but are not

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.319
as risky as a ‘basic deployment’.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.720
This is due to it being used for products that
have service or version dependencies, meaning

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.839
they will only work if they are up to date.

00:01:48.209 --> 00:01:51.970
In a multi-service deployment, all
the parts of the software are kept

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.720
separately, but updated at the same time.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.259
This type of deployment is,
however, slow to rollback and can be

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:01.719
difficult to manage, test and verify.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.260
Chris: Rather than doing a ‘full update’
at each deployment, a rolling deployment

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.230
updates an ‘instance’ of the previous
version of the software product.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.880
It will wait until it is ready before
redirecting users to the new version,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.110
therefore eliminating downtime.

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:24.549
If there is an issue such as a bug, the rolling
deployment can be stopped, so is easy to rollback.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.580
Leah: A canary deployment is used to test
a ‘new version’ of a software product

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.760
and can be easily rolled back to the
‘previous version’ if there is an issue.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:40.020
This strategy is used within both
‘blue-green’ and ‘A/B testing’.

00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:45.440
A new version [the canary] is tested
before the old instances are replaced.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.769
If it fails the readiness test, then the
‘canary instance’ is removed, and the

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.310
deployment would automatically be rolled back.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.079
A ‘canary release’ has the lowest
risk due to its element of control.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:05.190
Chris: An alternative strategy is to use A/B
testing which is also known as ‘A/B versions’ This

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:10.129
is where both versions are active at the same time
with different groups of users on each version.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:15.159
It can be used to test ‘interface
changes’ and ‘features’ to get ‘user

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.029
feedback’ and be used for ‘verification’.

00:03:18.789 --> 00:03:22.820
This strategy differs to the other
deployment strategies as its focus is

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.549
on ‘experimentation and exploration’.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:32.619
A/B testing is more about testing multiple ideas,
rather than deploying one specific tested idea.

